<p>The APA survey was conducted online by The Harris Poll among <strong>2,515 employed US adults, 17–27 April 2023</strong>, with weighting described in the report. Its findings are useful evidence about that surveyed population and period.</p>

<p>The Stanford account describes a meta-analysis examining workplace stressors such as long hours, job insecurity and work–family conflict. A modelled association should not be presented as a directly counted outcome or a precise forecast for another organisation.</p>

<h2 id="do-not-combine-figures-that-measure-different-things">Do not combine figures that measure different things</h2>

<p>The WHO productivity estimate and the Stanford healthcare estimate cover different populations, conditions, methods and periods. Adding them would not produce a defensible “total cost of workplace stress.”</p>

<p>Likewise, a percentage reporting stress is not the percentage with a diagnosed disorder. A historical estimate is not current spending. Keep these distinctions in presentations, procurement documents and learning-programme proposals.</p>

<h2 id="what-employers-can-act-on">What employers can act on</h2>

<p>WHO identifies risks including excessive workloads, low control, insecure work and discrimination. Its guidance covers organisational interventions, manager training, worker mental-health literacy, support and return-to-work arrangements.</p>

<p>Start with the conditions people work in. Training should support an appropriate organisational response rather than substitute for addressing workload, management practice or access to qualified help.</p>

<p>A learning programme can build assessed understanding and recognise completion. Wellbeing outcomes have a separate basis in organisational conditions, qualified support and appropriate outcome evidence.</p>

<h2 id="learning-within-a-wider-workplace-response">Learning within a wider workplace response</h2>

<p>Workplace training is one component of a broader response to stress. Its contribution depends on the conditions employees face, the support available and the capabilities being developed. Participation and assessed learning describe the programme; health outcomes describe a different result.</p>

<p>Employee wellbeing information requires appropriate privacy and access arrangements. Organisational changes also influence outcomes, so the effect of training is understood within that wider context.</p>

